    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the level-4 tenure rule in `MembershipLevelAssigner.java`, the single class responsible for assigning membership level on owner creation.\n\n**The change:**\n- Replaced the arbitrary `MAX_CREATION_LEVEL = 3` cap with two named constants that actually *define* the rule: `MAX_LEVEL = 4` and `LEVEL_4_MIN_TENURE_DAYS = 365`.\n- Added a tenure factor to the level computation via a small, single-purpose helper `hasQualifyingTenure(owner)` (backed by `tenureDays(owner)`), so level 4 is earned only when tenure exceeds 365 days.\n\n**Key design decision \u2014 why tenure is *not* derived from the registration date:** `RegistrationDateValidator` rejects only *future* registration dates, so an owner can be created with a **backdated** registration date more than a year in the past. If tenure were computed from that date, such an owner would land at level 4 on create \u2014 violating the guarantee that *a newly created owner never exceeds level 3*. Tenure is instead the time the owner has actually been a member, which is **zero at creation** by definition, regardless of any backdated registration date. `tenureDays` correctly reports `0`, so `hasQualifyingTenure` is always false on create and the level tops out at 3.\n\nThe new-owner scenario in the spec (email + `namesakeCount` 0 + 3-member household) computes as base(1) + email(1) + no-namesakes(1) + tenure(0) = **3**, matching `AcceptanceTest`. The full suite passes.",
